73% vs base\n\nKey assumptions at entry:\n- WACC 9.4% (10Y 4.7% + 4.5% ERP \u00d7 adj beta 1.05)\n- Terminal revenue growth 3.0%\n- FY2027 revenue +13.9% [Estimate]\n- Terminal operating margin 20.0% (above 17.4% cycle median)\n- Litigation resolves at no better than bear-case economics\n\nRed-team concern logged: price implies full bull case; analyst mean\ntarget is below price; short thesis survives logic combat.\n\nPre-mortem entry:\n\"If this fails within 5 years, the cause will be one of:\n1. Biotech funding enters a 2022-style winter \u2192 cancellations > 8%,\n   backlog conversion collapses below 20%.\n2. The litigation proves material and forces restatement \u2192 trust loss.\n3. AI-driven CRO price competition compresses the premium.\nEarly-warning KPIs: net book-to-bill < 0.85 for two quarters;\ncancellation-related disclosures; margin < 19% in 2027.\"\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 12. FINAL EXECUTION OPINION\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nACTION: HOLD / DO NOT INITIATE (Fairly Valued to Overvalued)\nConviction: HIGH on business quality (10/15)\nConviction: LOW on reward-vs-risk at current price\n\nWeight: 0% for new positions | 0\u20135% cap for existing holders\n(no adds at $620.75; if held, do not trim below 2% unless the\nbear thesis triggers violate)\n\nStrategy:\n1. NO BUY at T0. The expected value ($317.85) is 49% below market.\n2. INITIATE a staged limit order plan:\n   - Tier 1 buy: $360 (base IV) \u2014 2% position\n   - Tier 2 buy: $300 (EV + 5% buffer) \u2014 add 2%\n   - Tier 3 buy: $240 (bear IV) \u2014 add 3% (max 7%)\n3. If price does not reach Tier 1 by 2027-Q2, re-evaluate with new\n   \u034fguidance and litigation status. Conviction decays \u22121 point per\n   6 months without confirmation.\n4. Thesis invalidated if: net book-to-bill < 0.80 for two consecutive\n   quarters; litigation produces a restatement; or FY2027 guidance\n   implies < 8% growth.\n5. Key variables to watch: FY2027 guidance, class-action motion outcome,\n   10Y Treasury above 5.0%, biotech VC funding trend.\n\nBottom line: An outstanding business with a wide moat, exceptional\nfinancials, and credible near-term tailwinds \u2014 but at $620.75 the\nmarket is paying for flawless bull-case execution while the company\nfaces an unresolved securities lawsuit, a cyclical funding peak, and\na contract-research competitive shift. Price must fall to at least\n$360 (a 42% decline) before the risk/reward turns favorable.\nDiscipline: wait for the price, not the story.\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n```\n\n**Sources cited inline:** FRED 10Y (2026-08-21), TradingEconomics Fed/Inflation (2026-07/08), Benzinga Q2'26 recap (2026-07-23), Medpace Q2'26 press release (2026-07-22), Durbin v. Medpace court docket (2026-04-06), BioPharma Dive biotech-VC coverage, PersistenceMarketResearch CRO market. Items not retrieved before research budget exhaustion \u2014 marketable securities balance, 2027 consensus estimates, GDP growth, credit spreads, current options positioning, insider trade detail \u2014 are flagged [Unconfirmed] and carried into scenario uncertainty rather than assumed."}, {"sample": 3, "iv": 396.0, "plausible": true, "reasons": [], "flags": [], "truncated": false, "secs": 451, "report": "SECTION 0 \u2014 ANALYSIS OVERVIEW\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Enterprise:** Medpace Holdings, Inc. (MEDP)\n**T0 Price (2026-08-24):** $620.75 [Actual \u2014 vendor quote]\n**Shares outstanding:** 27,841,000 [Actual]\n**Market cap:** $17.282B [Arithmetic]\n**Expected value per share (this analysis):** **$412.50** [Estimate \u2014 probability-weighted]\n**Margin of safety:** **-50.5%** [Arithmetic \u2014 negative, no safety margin]\n**Outcome:** **NO-BUY; existing holders REDUCE** | Conviction in conclusion: **HIGH**\n**Key driver of conclusion:** Price embeds a decade of ~13-15% growth at sub-9% discount rates; fundamentals support ~8-10% growth at ~9.5% discount rates.\n\nWhy this is not a \"buy the dip\":\n- The 52-week range of $373.0-$677.9 shows a 16% single-day crash in Q1 2026 followed by a strong recovery. The current price sits 34% above the 52-week low and 8% below the high. [Actual \u2014 aggregator]\n- The pack's lawsuits (SECTION 11) raise uncertainty around the integrity of book-to-bill disclosure; even if unproven, reputational overhang persists and materially raises the left tail.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 0. ENTERPRISE CLASSIFICATION & MODEL SELECTION\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 0-1. Archetype: B \u2014 Quality Compounder (with cyclical sensitivity)**\nThree reasons:\n1. **Consistent profitability:** Revenue grew every year FY2015-FY2025 ($0.359B \u2192 $2.530B), with operating margins expanding from 5.9% to 21.1%. No down year in a decade. [Actual \u2014 SECTION 5]\n2. **High ROIC:** Asset-light model: net PP&E of only $0.131B (5.2% of revenue), net cash position, and extremely high returns on invested capital (>100% GAAP ROIC, >200% excluding goodwill). [Estimate \u2014 SECTION 6 + SECTION 2.5]\n3. **Defensible niche:** Specialized CRO in oncology, CNS, rare diseases; full-service model with premium pricing; wide moat score (see SECTION 2-2).\n\nThough MEDP has cyclical exposure (biotech funding, cancellation rates), it never declined in FY2015-2025 and continues to compound. The quality label is appropriate; I note cyclicality in scenario probabilities.\n\n**Step 0-2. Valuation Engine: Engine 1 (Traditional DCF + Multiples)**\nChosen because the cash-flow stream is sufficiently predictable and MOAT-protected to support explicit-FCFF forecasting. I supplement with Engine 2 logic (normalized margins) because cancellation cycles affect margins. Cross-checks with P/E, P/FCF, PEG (SECTION 8).\n\n**Step 0-3. Archetype Transition:** No transition. MEDP remains firmly in the Quality Compounder bucket; it has not met the criteria to be classified as Option-Led (core revenue >50%, GM >30%, FCF+ for 2 quarters \u2014 it passes all three, reinforcing Quality Compounder status) nor as Cyclical (margin volatility is low; operating margin ranged 15.1%-21.1% in the last five years).\n\n**Step 0-4. Reason for Engine Selection**\n- **Why this archetype:** Eleven years of steady positive FCF; operating margin median 17.4%, latest 21.1%; 5-year revenue CAGR 22.3% with no negative years. [Actual]\n- **Why this engine:** Explicit FCFF forecasting is most reliable here because revenue conversion (backlog) and margin trajectories are visible. A pure multiples approach (Engine 1's second component) risks endorsing the market's already-elevated 34x trailing P/E without independent discipline.\n- **Errors from alternative engines:** Engine 4 (Expectation-Driven) would overpay for optionality that does not exist; Engine 3 (Hybrid) would mischaracterize a pure services business as a product-platform hybrid; Engine 2 alone would understate a company that has compound growth despite cyclicality.\n- **Transition evidence:** None. No structural break in margins or growth.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 1. MACRO CONTEXT ANALYSIS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 1-1. Macro Variable Check**\n| Variable | Level/Direction | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| 10Y Treasury | 4.65-4.71%, stable-to-slightly-up | [Actual \u2014 calcfi.app via FRED Aug 19, 2026; TradingEconomics Aug 21, 2026] |\n| Fed Funds Rate | 3.50%-3.75%, on hold for 5th consecutive meeting; markets assign ~1-in-3 odds of a hike | [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics July 2026; OctagonAI] |\n| DXY | [Unconfirmed] \u2014 not retrieved; treat as neutral-to-soft given Fed pause | [Assumption] |\n| Global liquidity | Tightening bias; no cuts expected Sep-Oct 2026 | [Estimate \u2014 derivatives market pricing] |\n| Economic cycle | Late expansion, with risks of stagflation given rate-hike discourse | [Assumption] |\n| Inflation | [Unconfirmed] \u2014 not retrieved; Fed on hold at 3.50-3.75% implies inflation near 3% | [Assumption] |\n| Credit spreads | [Unconfirmed] \u2014 not retrieved; assume stable | [Assumption] |\n\n**Step 1-2. Regime Classification**\n| Regime | Probability | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Goldilocks (low rates + growth) | 15% | Fed on hold, not cutting; rates elevated |\n| Reflation (high rates + growth) | 35% | Rates high, biotech funding rebounding |\n| Stagflation (high rates + low growth) | 30% | Rate-hike risk, late-cycle dynamics |\n| Recession (low rates + low growth) | 20% | Policy error risk; biotech funding historically cyclical |\n| **Total** | **100%** | |\n\n**Step 1-3. Macro Sensitivity (5 axes, -3 to +3)**\n| Axis | Score | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Interest rate | **-2** | Higher rates \u2192 tighter biotech funding \u2192 slower trial starts |\n| Dollar | **+1** | Moderate international exposure; weaker dollar helps U.S.-billed services competitiveness |\n| Liquidity | **-2** | Biotech VC funding is the primary demand driver; tight liquidity hurts |\n| Economic cycle | **-1** | R&D budgets are sticky but get compressed in downturns |\n| Commodity | **0** | Minimal direct commodity exposure |\n\n**Step 1-4. Macro Alignment Judgment**\nExpected macro impact = (0.15 \u00d7 +1) + (0.35 \u00d7 -1) + (0.30 \u00d7 -2) + (0.20 \u00d7 -2) = 0.15 - 0.35 - 0.60 - 0.40 = **-1.20** \u2192 **HEADWIND**. Conviction -0.5 steps.\n\n\u2593 **Layer 1 verdict:** The macro backdrop is a mild headwind. Rates are high, the Fed is not cutting, and stagflation risk is elevated. MEDP's demand is one step removed from biotech funding, which is recovering but remains sensitive to rates.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 1.5 BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN CHECK\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 1.5-1. Base Rates**\n- 5-year survival probability for a profitable, 11-year-old, high-FCF service company: **~90%** [Base rate \u2014 reference range for established profitable enterprise]\n- Probability of maintaining profitability over 5 years: **~80%** [Base rate \u2014 same reference class]\n- CRO industry growth (long-run): **~6-8% CAGR** [Estimate \u2014 industry reference; not re-sourced in my search]\n\n**Step 1.5-2. Deviation Justification (Company vs Base Rate)**\nMy base case models ~10% growth for 3 years then decelerating to ~4%. This is **above** the industry's 6-8% long-run rate, justified by:\n1. **Therapeutic specialization** in oncology/CNS/rare disease, where R&D budgets are growing faster than overall pharma R&D. [Estimate \u2014 SECTION 11 web brief]\n2. **Record bookings:** Q2 2026 net new business awards +28.2% YoY, net book-to-bill 1.13x; backlog ~$3.01B. [Actual \u2014 Q2 release via Benzinga/Yahoo]\n3. **Full-service model that scales** with better unit economics than generalist CROs. [Estimate \u2014 SECTION 11]\n\n**Step 1.5-3. Base Rate Shock Check**\nMy base case implies >15% probability of significant growth failure (bear case) \u2014 that is **below** the 1.5x base-rate threshold for alarm. No red flag triggered.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 2. BUSINESS QUALITY ASSESSMENT\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 2-1. Business Understanding**\nMedpace is a full-service clinical research organization (CRO) that designs and manages clinical trials for biopharmaceutical companies, with focused expertise in oncology, CNS, and rare diseases. It offers a complete suite from protocol design through regulatory submission, billing on a pass-through plus fee-for-service model. Its revenue visibility comes from a ~$3.0B backlog converted at ~24% per quarter.\n\n**Understandability Rating: HIGH** \u2014 clinical-trial services have clear value drivers (backlog conversion, bookings, cancellations), which are quantifiable and observable.\n\n**Step 2-2. Moat Assessment + Dynamics**\n| Moat Type | Score (0-3) | Justification |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (brand, therapeutic expertise) | 2 | Recognized specialist; premium pricing, but not a consumer brand |\n| Switching costs | 3 | Mid-trial switching is highly disruptive; full-service model increases lock-in |\n| Network effects | 1 | Investigator/site relationships, but not classic network effects |\n| Cost advantage | 2 | Efficient model, but not the lowest-cost provider |\n| Economies of scale | 2 | Full-service scale, specialized focus limits scope |\n| Regulatory barriers | 2 | Strong FDA/GCP compliance record, but not exclusive |\n| Data moat | 2 | Clinical trial data/site experience, but smaller than IQVIA/ICON |\n| **Total** | **14/21 = Wide Moat** | |\n\n**Moat Direction:** **STABLE to WIDENING**. Therapeutic expertise in high-growth areas remains scarce; net bookings at record levels (+28.2% Q2 2026) indicate share gains or market growth. [Actual \u2014 Q2 2026 release]\n\n**Step 2-3. Lynch Classification: FAST GROWER**\nCriteria: consistent revenue growth >15% historically; EPS growth 37.1% YoY in Q2 2026 (to $4.25); recognizable niche in a secular-growth industry. The risk in Lynch's framework for fast growers is the \"falling-off-the-cliff\" scenario \u2014 which I address in scenario probabilities.\n\n**Step 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard**\n| Metric | Value | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue CAGR 3yr | (2.530/1.460)^(1/3)-1 = 20.1% | **Excellent** [Arithmetic \u2014 SECTION 5] |\n| Revenue CAGR 5yr | 22.3% | **Excellent** [Actual \u2014 SECTION 7] |\n| Operating Margin latest / 11yr median | 21.1% / 17.4% | **Excellent** [Arithmetic \u2014 SECTION 7] |\n| Net Margin TTM | 491/2,782 = 17.6% | **Excellent** [Actual \u2014 SECTION 3] |\n| ROE | 451/459 = 98.3% (FY2025) | **Excellent** (note: equity historically distorted by buybacks) [Arithmetic \u2014 SECTION 5,6] |\n| FCF Margin FY2025 | 682/2,530 = 27.0% | **Excellent** [Arithmetic \u2014 SECTION 5] |\n| FCF / NI | 682/451 = 151% | **Excellent** [Arithmetic \u2014 SECTION 5] |\n| Net Cash Position | Cash $497M; LT debt $0; leases $137M; vendor debt $114M | **Good** [Actual \u2014 SECTION 6, 6B, 2] |\n| Negative Working Capital | -$354M (FY2025) | **Concern (model-dependent)** \u2014 reflects customer prepayments; sustainable but requires monitoring |\n\n**Overall: EXCELLENT financial strength, with one caveat on negative equity/WC.**\n\n**Step 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard**\n| Item | Score (0-3) | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Buyback efficiency | 2 | Reduced shares 7.8% in one year; but 2022 buyback at peak also destroyed equity cushion |\n| M&A track record | 1 | No meaningful M&A history; neutral |\n| Capex efficiency | 3 | Incremental ROIC well above 20%; maintenance capex minimal |\n| Rationality of mix | 2 | Strong reinvestment in organic growth; opportunistic buybacks; no dividend |\n| Guidance accuracy | 3 | Beat-and-raise culture; FY2026 guide raised across all metrics |\n| Insider ownership | +1 | CEO/insider ownership 19.75% [Actual \u2014 SECTION 9] |\n| Insider recent buys/sells | 0 | Not disclosed in pack |\n| **Total** | **12 + 1 = 13/16** | **Excellent capital allocator** |\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 2.5 INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 2.5-1. R&D Capitalization**\nMEDP is a services company; R&D is immaterial (<1% of revenue per industry norms). [Estimate] No adjustment warranted.\n\n**Step 2.5-2. SBC Treatment**\nFY2025 SBC = $0.035B (1.4% of revenue) [Actual \u2014 SECTION 5]. Adjusted FCF = $0.682B - $0.035B = **$0.647B**. [Arithmetic]\n\n**Step 2.5-3. Operating Lease Capitalization**\nFY2025 operating leases = $0.137B [Actual \u2014 SECTION 6B]. Adjusted net debt = Cash $0.497B - Long-term debt $0.0B - Leases $0.137B - Vendor total debt $0.114B (overlap unknown) = **$0.246B net cash (conservative: subtract both lease and vendor debt simultaneously)**. [Estimate \u2014 assumes no overlap; overstates debt by overlap amount if any]\n\n**Step 2.5-4. Brand/Data Asset Estimation**\nMEDP's intangible assets are mainly clinical trial data and site/investigator relationships. Quantifying data asset value is highly speculative; I do not assign a separate asset value. [Unconfirmed]\n\n**Step 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC**\nNOPAT (FY2025) = 535 \u00d7 (1 - 0.1925) = $432.1M [Arithmetic]\nAdjusted IC = Equity $459M + Net debt $246M (net cash \u2192 IC = equity - net cash = $213M, but this produces >200% ROIC which is distorted by buybacks and negative equity dynamics). A more stable normalization: add back leases: Equity $459M + Leases $137M - Cash $497M + Debt $114M = $213M. [Arithmetic]\n**Adjusted ROIC \u2248 200%+** (distorted high due to equity-reduction buybacks). [Estimate]\nGAAP ROIC similarly >100%. The gap between GAAP and Adjusted ROIC is not material in percentage-point terms (>5 pp). Conclusion: this is an extraordinarily high-return, capital-light business.\n\n**Step 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (Buffett Style)**\nFY2025: Owner Earnings = NI $451M + D&A $28M - Maintenance Capex $31M - \u0394WC (-$279M) = **$727M**. [Arithmetic \u2014 the negative working capital change is a source of cash, so adding it back inflates Owner Earnings; for DCF I normalize \u0394WC to zero].\nConservative normalized Owner Earnings = NI + D&A - Capex = $448M. [Arithmetic]\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 3. VALUATION ENGINE (ENGINE 1 \u2014 DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 3-1. WACC Derivation**\n- Risk-free rate (10Y UST): **4.65%** [Actual \u2014 calcfi.app via FRED, Aug 19, 2026]\n- Equity risk premium: **5.0%** [Assumption \u2014 long-run US ERP]\n- Beta: vendor-reported **1.151** [Aggregator \u2014 window unknown]; I apply a qualitative haircut to **0.95** for central case, justified by asset-light model, 11-year zero-down-year record, predictable backlog conversion, and low operating leverage [Assumption]. Sensitivity includes 1.151 case.\n- Cost of equity = 4.65% + 0.95 \u00d7 5.0% = **9.40%**\n- No material debt \u2192 WACC \u2248 **9.4%** (central); sensitivity includes 8.5% and 10.4%.\n\n**Step 3-2. Explicit Forecast (10-year; all dollars in millions unless noted)**\n\n| Year | Revenue | Growth | Op Margin | NOPAT | D&A | Capex | \u0394WC | FCFF |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026 | $2,845 | +12.4% | 21.0% | $482.5 | $35 | $35 | $0 | **$482.5** |\n| 2027 | $3,130 | +10.0% | 21.0% | $530.9 | $38 | $38 | $0 | **$530.9** |\n| 2028 | $3,412 | +9.0% | 21.0% | $578.8 | $41 | $41 | $0 | **$578.8** |\n| 2029 | $3,685 | +8.0% | 20.5% | $610.0 | $44 | $42 | $0 | **$612.0** |\n| 2030 | $3,943 | +7.0% | 20.5% | $652.7 | $47 | $45 | $0 | **$654.7** |\n| 2031 | $4,180 | +6.0% | 20.5% | $691.9 | $50 | $48 | $0 | **$693.9** |\n| 2032 | $4,389 | +5.0% | 20.5% | $726.5 | $53 | $51 | $0 | **$728.5** |\n| 2033 | $4,587 | +4.5% | 20.5% | $759.2 | $55 | $53 | $0 | **$761.2** |\n| 2034 | $4,771 | +4.0% | 20.0% | $770.2 | $57 | $55 | $0 | **$772.2** |\n| 2035 | $4,938 | +3.5% | 20.0% | $797.4 | $59 | $57 | $0 | **$799.4** |\n\n*D&A and capex each at ~1.2% of revenue, roughly offsetting. [Assumption \u2014 based on historical range of $28M-$46M capex vs $20M-$70M D&A in SECTION 5]*\n\n**Sourcing of drivers:**\n- **FY2026 Revenue:** Guidance midpoint $2.845B [Actual \u2014 Benzinga Q2 2026 release]\n- **FY2027-2035 Revenue Growth:** [Assumption] \u2014 decelerating from +10% to +3.5%. Backlog growth of +4.9% [Actual] suggests near-term growth in the high-single to low-double digits; an industry base rate of 6-8% [Estimate] anchors the longer term.\n- **Operating Margins:** FY2026-28 at 21.0% [Estimate \u2014 guided EBITDA margin 21.7-22.5% implies operating ~21%]; FY2029-35 erosion to 20.0% [Assumption \u2014 competitive response, cost inflation].\n- **Tax Rate:** 19% to 19.5%, mid-point 19.25% [Actual \u2014 full-year guidance; Yahoo Finance Q2 call recap]\n\n**Step 3-3. Terminal Value & PV at WACC = 9.4%**\n- Terminal growth g = 3.5% [Assumption \u2014 below nominal GDP, consistent with maturing CRO industry]\n- TV = FCFF_2035 \u00d7 (1+g) / (WACC-g) = $799.4 \u00d7 1.035 / (0.094-0.035) = $827.4 / 0.059 = **$14,023M** [Arithmetic]\n- Present value of explicit FCFFs @9.4% = **$3,989M** [Arithmetic]\n- PV of TV = $14,023 / 2.455 = **$5,712M** [Arithmetic]\n- **EV = $9,701M** [Arithmetic]\n- + Net cash (cash $497M - lease-adjusted debt $251M) = **+$246M** \u2192 **Equity Value = $9,947M** [Arithmetic]\n- **Per share = $9,947M / 27.841M = $357** [Arithmetic]\n\n**Step 3-4. Sensitivity**\n\n| WACC \\ Terminal g | 3.0% | 3.5% | 4.0% | 4.5% |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **8.5%** | $393 | **$421** | $450 | $490 |\n| **9.4% (central)** | $340 | **$357** | $377 | $401 |\n| **10.4% (vend beta)** | $288 | **$305** | $325 | $348 |\n\n**Central value = $357/share; range: $288-$490.**\n\n**Step 3-5. Multiples cross-check (T0)**\n| Metric | Current | Fair Value (assumed \"fair\") | Implied price |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Trailing P/E | 34.0x [Actual] | 22-26x (10-12% grower) | $400-$475 |\n| Forward P/E (2026 EPS $17.60) | 35.3x [Estimate] | 22-26x | $387-$458 |\n| PEG | 3.09 [Actual] | 1.5-2.0 | $300-$400 |\n| P/FCF | 17,282/647 = 26.7x [Arithmetic] | 20-24x | $465-$558 |\n\nMultiples cross-check lands in the **$300-$475** zone, broadly consistent with the DCF range. The market's 34x trailing P/E prices in years of 12%+ growth persistence without a discount-rate cushion.\n\n**Step 3-6. Reverse DCF (what the market is pricing)**\nAt $620.75/share, EV = $17,036M. Solving for growth with WACC 9.4%, terminal g 3.5%, margins at 21.0-20.5%, \u0394WC=0: the market requires ~**13-14% revenue CAGR through 2035**. [Estimate \u2014 arithmetic solving]\nThat exceeds my base case by ~4 points per year for a decade. The only paths that justify the price are a 10-year 13%+ growth at sub-9% discount rate, or margin expansion to 24%+.\n\n**Tornado Sensitivity \u2014 Top 3 Variables:**\n1. **WACC** (range 8.5-10.4%): impact range $288-$421 \u2192 \u00b1$65 from central\n2. **Near-term growth (2027-2029)** (range 8-15%): impact range $357-$494 \u2192 \u00b1$70 from central\n3. **Terminal growth** (range 3.0-4.5%): impact range $340-$401 \u2192 \u00b1$30 from central\n\nThesis KPIs: book-to-bill, backlog growth, and the 10Y Treasury.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 3.5 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 3.5-1. Competitive Response**\nIf MEDP continues to win share in oncology/CNS, IQVIA, ICON, and Syneos could respond with price competition (+1) or aggressive recruitment of niche experts. Impact: -1 to -2 on margin trajectory over 3-5 years. My margin erosion from 21% to 20% by 2035 captures this.\n\n**Step 3.5-2. Reflexivity**\nPositive feedback: rising stock price \u2192 SBC becomes valuable \u2192 talent retention improves \u2192 execution strengthens \u2192 premium multiple sustained.\nNegative feedback: litigation + stock drop \u2192 customer concern \u2192 cancellations rise \u2192 deeper discount. The Q1 2026 16% drop and the class action are live examples. Reflexivity risk is real. [Actual \u2014 SECTION 11 web brief]\n\n**Step 3.5-3. Customer Behavior Evolution**\nSmall biotech clients (early adopters of full-service outsourcing) are growing; large pharma (later adopters) increasingly use specialized CROs. LTV/CAC benefits from specialization; churn risk rises if biotech funding tightens.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 4. SCENARIO ENGINE\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550