ion rejected. Weighted macro environment: mildly reflationary-tight.\n\nStep 1-3. Macro sensitivity matrix (\u20133 to +3 for MEDP)\n  Interest-rate sensitivity: \u20132 (biotech clients fund via capital markets; higher rates raise their cost of capital and pressure cancellations).\n  Dollar sensitivity: \u20131 (mostly US-dollar contracts; modest non-US cost base).\n  Liquidity sensitivity: \u20132 (VC/biotech funding is the marginal dollar for small/mid biotech sponsors).\n  Economic-cycle sensitivity: +1 (healthcare R&D is more defensive than cyclicals, but R&D budgets do flex).\n  Commodity sensitivity: 0.\n  Composite: mildly negative (\u20134/15).\n\nStep 1-4. Macro alignment\n  Regime distribution \u00d7 sensitivity \u2192 Expected macro impact: \u20130.55 [Estimate]. Verdict: MILD HEADWIND.\n  Action: Conviction \u20130.5 steps; entry strategy must be staggered, not lumpy; position-sizing caps apply.\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1.5-1. Base rates applicable to MEDP\n  \u2013 Established, profitable, 11-year operating CRO: 5-year survival probability ~90% [Assumption based on sector history].\n  \u2013 Sustaining >20% operating margin for 5 more years: ~45\u201350% [Assumption; industry-median CRO margins 12\u201318%, MEDP 21.1% is a positive outlier].\n  \u2013 Sustaining >10% revenue CAGR for 5 more years after a deceleration from 32% to ~5% implied H2 growth: ~30% [Estimate].\n  \u2013 Specialty therapeutic CROs maintaining pricing premium vs generalists: ~60% [Estimate].\n  \u2013 Large-cap-quality healthcare services at 35x forward earnings producing >10% annual returns over next 5 years: ~25\u201330% [Assumption, historical multiple-mean-reversion base rate].\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from base rate required to justify the market price\n  T0 price embeds roughly 10+ years of double-digit FCF growth and re-acceleration (reverse-DCF, Section 4.5). That exceeds my success base rate by >1.5x. Company-specific justifications for deviation: (1) record Q2-2026 net bookings and RFPs up sequentially & YoY [Actual]; (2) all-equity balance sheet with net cash, no refinancing risk [Actual]; (3) founder-led, 19.75% insider ownership \u2014 alignment unusually strong [Actual]. These justify 1.2\u20131.4x base rate, not >1.5x. Red flag triggered.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base rate shock check\n  Market-implied success probability >2.0x base rate. MANDATORY RE-EVALUATION applied \u2014 this is the core reason for the SELL verdict: the price already capitalizes an outcome meaningfully better than the base rate permits.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY ASSESSMENT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2-1. Business understanding\n  Medpace is a full-service clinical research organization (CRO) that designs and runs clinical trials for biopharma sponsors, concentrated in oncology, rare disease, metabolic, and CNS. Revenue is recognized as trials progress; backlog (un-performed services under contract) is the primary forward-visibility asset. Understandability: HIGH (simple service/backlog conversion model).\n\nStep 2-2. Moat assessment + dynamics (0\u20133 each)\n  Intangible assets (therapeutic expertise, KOL relationships): 3 \u2014 narrowing slowly as AI-assisted trial design commoditizes elements.\n  Switching costs (mid-trial provider changes are very disruptive): 3 \u2014 stable.\n  Network effects: 1 \u2014 minimal; data moat partially offsets.\n  Cost advantage: 2 \u2014 no physical scale advantage, but efficient delivery model.\n  Economies of scale: 2 \u2014 full-service model benefits from scale; diluted by specialization.\n  Regulatory barriers: 1 \u2014 low; FDA/cross-border expertise helps but is not exclusive.\n  Data moat: 2 \u2014 historical trial data is a real but not dominant asset.\n  Total: 14/21 \u2192 WIDE MOAT, direction: STABLE-TO-NARROWING. Narrowing signs: generalist CROs (IQVIA, Syneos) competing on integrated data/AI; prolonged award-to-revenue timelines; pricing pressure risk flagged in SWOT sources [Estimate, web research brief].\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch classification\n  FAST GROWER transitioning to STALWART. Core checkpoint: growth rate. At the implied H2-2026 ~4.6% YoY, the fast-grower classification fails its own test \u2014 must be monitored each quarter. A fast grower must sustain >10%; below that, de-rate.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial strength scorecard [Actual unless noted]\n  Revenue CAGR (5-yr, FY2021\u2013FY2025): +22.3% [Arithmetic].\n  FY2025 operating margin: 21.1%; 11-yr median: 17.4%.\n  FY2025 net income: $451M; EPS: $15.28 diluted.\n  ROE (vendor): ~162% \u2014 driven by a tiny, buyback-depleted equity base ($459M equity) [Arithmetic].\n  ROIC (GAAP): effectively undefined/infinite because net invested capital \u2248 \u2212$38M (equity $459M \u2212 cash $497M) [Arithmetic].\n  FCF conversion: FY2025 FCF $682M vs NI $451M = 151% \u2014 FLAWED metric because FY2025 included a ~$279M working-capital release [Arithmetic]. Normalized FCF power $400\u2013450M [Estimate].\n  Net debt: net cash \u2248 $360M (cash $497M \u2212 operating leases $137M, no long-term debt filed in FY2022\u201325) [Estimate]. Leverage immaterial.\n  Judgment: EXCELLENT structural quality, but recent FCF conversion is NOT repeatable \u2014 do not extrapolate 150% conversion.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard (0\u20133 each)\n  Buyback efficiency: 1 (aggressive buybacks at 30\u201340x earnings; EPS boost real, timing rich).\n  M&A track record: 3 (no meaningful M&A; no value destruction).\n  Capex efficiency: 3 (asset-light; incremental ROIC extremely high).\n  Rationality of mix: 3 (all buyback, no dividend; appropriate for a high-ROIC asset-light company).\n  Guidance accuracy: 3 (consistent beat-and-raise record; FY2026 raised again).\n  Insider ownership bonus: +1 (19.75% insider ownership [Actual]; no cluster selling observed in pack).\n  Total: 14/15 \u2014 EXCELLENT capital allocator, with the single flaw of buying back stock at peak multiple.\n\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2.5-1. R&D capitalization: not meaningful \u2014 CRO spends on delivery, not product R&D. OI adjusted: $535M + $35M SBC \u2212 ~$12M amortization (3-yr useful life) \u2248 $558M [Estimate].\nStep 2.5-2. SBC proper treatment: FY2025 SBC $35M [Actual]. Adjusted owner FCF = GAAP FCF $682M \u2212 SBC $35M \u2212 normalized WC usage \u2248 $430\u2013450M [Estimate]. TTM adjusted FCF \u2248 $672M (does NOT include normalized WC).\nStep 2.5-3. Operating lease capitalization: $137M operating leases [Actual] added to debt for net-debt purposes: net cash \u2248 $360M.\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/data asset: not priced into model (immaterial for a service business).\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC: Adjusted NOPAT \u2248 $558M \u00d7 (1\u22120.21) \u2248 $441M; Adjusted invested capital \u2248 \u2212$38M + $60M unamortized SBC [Assumption] \u2248 $22M \u